From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH 65/65] cuda/hip: name the pre-add cross-block hazard and guard it

Round 50 shipped the residual fold at one row and left the multi-row path
declined, with the defect localised to "a cross-block interaction". This
identifies it exactly and re-enables the multi-row fold behind a host-side
guard.

### The nrows ladder: it is not "more than one block", it is more than one WAVE

Same binary, the runner's gate command, fold forced on, 5 loads per rung:

    nrows    1     3.9382 x6                                        0.000%
    nrows    2     3.9137 x5                                        0.000%
    nrows    8     3.9184 x5                                        0.000%
    nrows   64     3.9619 x5                                        0.000%
    nrows  512     3.9368 3.9353 3.9353 3.9353 3.9350   3 distinct  0.046%

64 blocks on 64 CUs are co-resident and issue every loop-1 load before any
loop-2 store, so the window never opens. 512 blocks run in several dispatch
waves and it does.

### The hazard, from the ranges rather than from reasoning

`GGML_RMS_PRE_ADD_AUDIT=1` dumps every fold's write and read ranges. **9 of 79
folds per graph** look like this:

    dst     = 0x...204600 +16384   ->  [0x204600, 0x208600)
    add_dst = 0x...200000 +16384   ->  [0x200000, 0x204000)
    src0    = 0x...200000 +16384   ==  add_dst   (in-place, safe)
    src1    = 0x...204680 +16384   ->  [0x204680, 0x208680)

`dst` overlaps `src1` with the bases **128 bytes apart** - not a whole number of
the 8192-byte row stride. So block r's loop-2 store into `dst` row r lands on
the last 32 floats of `src1` row **r-1**, which block r-1 reads in its loop 1.
Nothing orders those two, and 32 of 2048 floats is exactly the size of
perturbation the perplexity jitter has always shown.

This is the overlap round 45 enumerated and dismissed as "benign by
construction because each block owns one row". Each block does own one row -
of its own tensors. It does not own the row of a differently-based tensor it
happens to overlap.

### The guard

`ggml_cuda_pre_add_write_safe`: for each writer (`dst`, `add_dst`) against each
reader (every add operand), an overlap is only safe when the two tensors start
at exactly the same address - the in-place residual add the kernel already
assumes. Any other overlap, including one a whole number of rows apart,
declines the fold. Applied only above one row, so the round-50 decode path is
untouched by construction.

    gate command, guard ON (shipped default), 8 loads
      3.9318 x8                                      spread 0.000%
    gate command, guard OFF (GGML_CUDA_PRE_ADD_NORM_UNSAFE=1), 8 loads
      3.9301 3.9339 3.9318 3.9322 3.9318 3.9318 3.9321 3.9297
      six distinct values                            spread 0.107%

3.9318 is the parent's own reading, so the 70 folds that survive the guard are
bit-identical to the add chain they replace, as designed.

### Timing

One binary, ABBA over 10 arms, `llama-bench -r 5`, `GGML_CUDA_PRE_ADD_NORM_ONE_ROW=1`
for the round-50 control:

    multi pp512  4967.3 4923.1 4893.2 4894.8 4877.3   mean 4911.1
    one   pp512  4924.5 4884.9 4856.9 4826.8 4835.5   mean 4865.7   -> +0.93%
    multi tg128  mean 164.80    one tg128  mean 164.87              -> -0.04%

5/5 adjacent arm pairs positive on prefill. Decode is flat by construction: the
guard only runs above one row, and decode is always one row.

**This is worth about +0.31% of score** (0.20 x 0.93% on prefill, 0.15 x 0.84%
on ttft) - below this track's practical bar and below the 0.75% confirmation
tolerance, so it is not submitted on its own. It ships with the next decode
lever, and it closes a defect that has cost five rounds and is latent in the
six sibling tracks that carry the same fold.

Toggles: `GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_PRE_ADD_NORM=1` parks the fold entirely;
`GGML_CUDA_PRE_ADD_NORM_ONE_ROW=1` is the round-50 build;
`GGML_CUDA_PRE_ADD_NORM_UNSAFE=1` drops the guard and reproduces the defect;
`GGML_RMS_PRE_ADD_AUDIT=1` dumps the ranges the guard tests.
